<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan José Flores</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan José Flores was a Venezuelan-born general of the independence wars who governed the southern district of Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>. When it broke away in 1830 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, he became the new republic's first president and annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a> in 1832. He dominated Ecuadorian politics until he was driven out by the revolution of 1845.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-jose-flores.jpg" alt="Juan José Flores" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Banrepcultural · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fl%C3%B3rez,_Juan_Jos%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan José Flores was born on 19 July 1800 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>, Venezuela, the illegitimate and only son of a Spanish merchant and a Venezuelan woman, Rita Flores Bohorques. His father returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and left mother and son in poverty. Flores had little formal education, and at fifteen he enlisted as a private in the royalist army of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-morillo">Pablo Morillo</a>.</p><p>Taken prisoner as a sergeant on 31 October 1817, he joined the patriot army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>. Bolívar noted his courage at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carabobo">Battle of Carabobo</a> in 1821, and at twenty-three Flores became a colonel and then commandant general of the freed city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasto">Pasto</a>. He was self-taught and read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, and the professors of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> gave him an honorary doctorate in 1842.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 13 May 1830, the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> broke away from Gran Colombia, Flores was named supreme chief of the new country, and on 14 August provisional president. The assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riobamba">Riobamba</a> elected him constitutional president, and his term began on 22 September 1830 and lasted until 10 September 1834. It was marked by turmoil: he faced a rebellion led by Luis Urdaneta, who wanted to keep Ecuador in Gran Colombia, and a threat from Vicente Rocafuerte, with whom he agreed that Rocafuerte would be president next and Flores head of the army. He also fought an invading Colombian army in 1832 and again from 1834, defeating it at Miñarica, near Ambato, on 18 January 1835.</p><p>His second term ran from 1 February 1839 to 15 January 1843. After irregularities in the elections of 1842 he had the 1835 constitution annulled, and the new constitution, nicknamed the Carta de la esclavitud, let him begin a third term on 1 April 1843. A rebellion led by Rocafuerte and Vicente Ramón Roca overthrew him on 6 March 1845.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Flores remained active in Ecuadorian military and political life. He commanded Ecuadorian forces at the Battle of Cuaspud on 6 December 1863, where a defeat weakened the army and thwarted his and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-garcia-moreno">Gabriel García Moreno</a>'s efforts to strengthen state centralisation. In 1864 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> to fight an expedition led by the former president José María Urvina in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guayaquil">Gulf of Guayaquil</a>. At the Battle of Santa Rosa he received a stray shot in the lower abdomen and died on 1 October aboard the steamer Smirck, near Puná Island. He is often called the Founder of the Republic, and Floreana Island in the Galápagos Archipelago is named for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan José Flores” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military commanders became powerful regional figures, among them José Antonio Páez in Venezuela and <b>Juan José Flores</b> in Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Venezuelan-born general <b>Juan José Flores</b>, who had married into the Quito aristocracy, became its first president.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in Puná Island, in what is now <a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.47,-68.02&amp;year=1841" aria-label="Puerto Cabello on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juan José Flores"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4328 4110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4328 4110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.47,-68.02&amp;year=1841">What was here in 1841? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
